  Keeps the members of the value tuple types that a value tuple query materializes through reflection.

  WHY THIS FILE IS NECESSARY

  A C# value tuple with more than seven fields is represented by the runtime as a NESTED value tuple: the
  eighth type argument of System.ValueTuple`8 is itself a value tuple holding the remaining fields.
  Materializing one therefore has to discover the fields and the constructor of a type that is only reached
  through the generic arguments of another type at run time.

  [DynamicallyAccessedMembers] cannot express that. The annotation on the query methods' type parameter
  preserves the members of the OUTERMOST value tuple type; it says nothing about the nested one, and
  annotations are not recursive. Without this file, publishing an application with Native AOT (or trimming it)
  removes the nested value tuple's fields and constructor, and a query for a value tuple with more than seven
  fields fails at run time. That was measured by the Native AOT smoke test, and it is why this file exists.

  WHY THIS IS NOT A SUPPRESSION

  A descriptor PRESERVES members; it does not silence a diagnostic. It is the sanctioned instrument for exactly
  this case - reflection over types the trimmer cannot reason about statically, whose members are nonetheless
  guaranteed to be there. The project rule is to make the reflection safe rather than to suppress the warning
  that reports it; this file is the "make it safe" half.

  THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING FOR THE PUBLIC API

  It is also what lets the generic query methods carry no [RequiresUnreferencedCode], and therefore what keeps
  a consumer's trimmed or Native AOT publish free of IL2026. ValueTupleMaterializerFactory.GetValueTupleConstructors
  suppresses one IL2065 and names this file as the justification, so deleting or shortening the list below does
  not merely regress a feature - it makes that suppression untrue. If you need to change it, read the comment at
  that method first; restoring [RequiresUnreferencedCode] on the query methods is the correct response, not
  leaving a suppression in place that no longer holds.

  COST

  The fields and constructors of eight small runtime types, kept in every trimmed application that references
  DbConnectionPlus. Preserving only the fields would not be enough, because the constructors are what
  materialization invokes.
-->

<linker>
	<assembly fullname="System.Private.CoreLib">
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`1" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`2" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`3" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`4" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`5" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`6" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`7" preserve="all" />
		<type fullname="System.ValueTuple`8" preserve="all" />
	</assembly>
</linker>
